## Algorithms implemented from published descriptions

**RotSprite**, in `apps/web/src/lib/transform/transform.ts`. A rotation
algorithm for small sprites, published by Xenowhirl and independently
documented. Implemented from the algorithm description. No code was taken from
any existing implementation.

## Studied, not copied

**[PixelRefiner](https://github.com/HappyOnigiri/PixelRefiner)** (MIT,
© 2026 Happy Onigiri) solves in-browser raster-to-pixel-art conversion, and its
approach informed the pixelisation pipeline in `apps/web/src/lib/pixelize/`.
**No code was ported.** Where this project diverges it does so deliberately —
notably by resolving each cell directly to a palette index rather than to RGB
followed by quantisation. The MIT notice is recorded here because the
techniques were studied, which the project's own rules require even absent a
line of copied code.

**[Aseprite](https://www.aseprite.org/)** is source-available under a
restrictive EULA, not open source. Feature *concepts* — pixel-perfect strokes,
shading ink, onion skin, tiled preview — are category conventions and are
implemented independently. No Aseprite source was read or copied.

**[Lospec](https://lospec.com/)** publishes palettes under no blanket licence,
with named authors. **No Lospec palette is bundled.** The six palette export
formats match the ones Lospec offers, because that is what the ecosystem reads.

Built-in palettes are hardware and fantasy-console data — Game Boy DMG and
PICO-8 — which is factual and not authored by us.

## Not used

`use-webmcp-tool` (Google Chrome Labs, Apache-2.0) was evaluated and briefly a
dependency. It is no longer used: the registration lifecycle is implemented
directly in `apps/web/src/lib/webmcp/` so that both `document.modelContext` and
the older `navigator.modelContext` binding are supported. It is listed here only
to record that it was removed rather than absorbed.
